1
00:00:00,190 --> 00:00:02,890
Greetings, aliens and earthlings!

2
00:00:02,890 --> 00:00:07,720
This is not an episode of Earthlings 101,
your crash course for alien visitors, but

3
00:00:07,720 --> 00:00:12,059
a series of three shorter videos about something
that seems to confuse earthlings and aliens

4
00:00:12,059 --> 00:00:12,639
alike:

5
00:00:12,639 --> 00:00:19,639
The shape of the universe.

6
00:00:42,960 --> 00:00:47,679
Most earthlings and aliens don't understand
space. It's not what they think it is.

7
00:00:47,679 --> 00:00:52,969
People assume that the universe is infinite
and flat. But actually, from a non-euklidean,

8
00:00:52,969 --> 00:00:57,210
non-local viewpoint, it's more like a big
ball of wibbly wobbly, spacey wacey stuff.

9
00:00:57,210 --> 00:01:00,069
And the shape of this big ball - that's what
this three-part

10
00:01:00,069 --> 00:01:02,229
series is all about.

11
00:01:02,229 --> 00:01:07,680
When we talk about "shape", we are basically
talking about two things : Topology and Geometry.

12
00:01:07,680 --> 00:01:12,160
Geometry is all about distances, angles, areas
and so on.

13
00:01:12,160 --> 00:01:16,420
Topology doesn't care about exact angles and
distances - it's about which zones are next

14
00:01:16,420 --> 00:01:17,540
to each other.

15
00:01:17,540 --> 00:01:22,380
For example, when you deform a square, you
change the geometry, but not the topology,

16
00:01:22,380 --> 00:01:26,390
as long as you don't rip it apart, create
holes, or glue parts together.

17
00:01:26,390 --> 00:01:30,990
For the sake of simplicity, let's neglect
the expansion of the universe and only consider

18
00:01:30,990 --> 00:01:34,380
a "snapshot" of the universe, i.e. a slice
of space time.

19
00:01:34,380 --> 00:01:39,030
Unfortunately, I don't know with certitude
what the actual shape of this slice is.

20
00:01:39,030 --> 00:01:43,130
All research on cosmo-topology is classified
as "secret of defense" by the Central Galactic

21
00:01:43,130 --> 00:01:43,830
Bureaucracy.

22
00:01:43,830 --> 00:01:49,310
So, I can only discuss hypotheses - different
possible models of the universe.

23
00:01:49,310 --> 00:01:53,750
Basically there are three kinds of models:
Spherical, flat, and hyperbolical.

24
00:01:53,750 --> 00:01:56,300
Today we will talk about flat models.

25
00:01:56,300 --> 00:01:59,640
Flat or not - there are a lot of misconceptions
about the universe around:

26
00:01:59,640 --> 00:02:05,020
"The universe, also, on the other hand, might
be finite: Somewhere out, beyond where we

27
00:02:05,020 --> 00:02:08,550
can see, there might be an edge."

28
00:02:08,550 --> 00:02:15,550
No, Hank! A finite universe, and a universe
with an edge, are two wholly different things.

29
00:02:15,990 --> 00:02:23,340
"ʘ○┘┌└ⱶ─┐ʘⱶ┘─∆○ʘⱷ⃝∙○†○◦┘⌐┐ⱶ⃝ʘ"

30
00:02:24,030 --> 00:02:29,230
No, Gorf. "Curved space" doesn't mean that
the universe curves through an embedding hyperspace.

31
00:02:29,230 --> 00:02:33,030
It only means that the laws of geometry are
not what you would expect.

32
00:02:33,240 --> 00:02:39,380
"10100001010101010101010101010"

33
00:02:39,480 --> 00:02:42,569
It doesn't expand into anything - it just
expands.

34
00:02:42,569 --> 00:02:47,819
'So, within the limits of our observational
capabilities to these days we think that the

35
00:02:47,819 --> 00:02:52,700
universe is flat. So you go out in one direction,
you probably shouldn't return the way you

36
00:02:52,700 --> 00:02:55,459
came. So, no, the universe doesn't appear
to be closed,

37
00:02:55,459 --> 00:02:58,459
and you'll probably never see the light coming
up behind you."

38
00:02:58,459 --> 00:03:04,150
No, Derek. A flat universe isn't necessarily
infinite, and can contain straight lines that

39
00:03:04,150 --> 00:03:06,020
lead back to their origin.

40
00:03:06,120 --> 00:03:15,020
'Blblllbll bblblblbl rrrrrrrrrr! bblblblb rrrrhhhr! rrrrrrrrrrrrrrr!"

41
00:03:15,020 --> 00:03:18,240
To be honest... That's just stupid.

42
00:03:18,340 --> 00:03:22,290
So, what does "finite universe" mean, anyway?

43
00:03:22,290 --> 00:03:24,520
It means that the universe has finite volume.

44
00:03:24,520 --> 00:03:29,380
In other words, you'd need only a finite amount
of hydric acid to inundate the whole universe.

45
00:03:29,380 --> 00:03:34,590
"No edge", on the other hand, means that when
you explore the universe, you won't suddenly

46
00:03:34,590 --> 00:03:39,209
run into a wall of nothingness - an edge.

47
00:03:39,209 --> 00:03:40,610
But isn't that the same?

48
00:03:40,610 --> 00:03:45,250
When there is no edge, you can just fly on
and on, and boldly go where no man or alien

49
00:03:45,250 --> 00:03:46,630
has gone before.

50
00:03:46,630 --> 00:03:52,510
And when you do so, you see more and more
volume, so the total volume must be infinite...

51
00:03:52,510 --> 00:03:54,099
Right?

52
00:03:54,099 --> 00:03:59,020
Wrong. Because when you fly on long enough,
you might eventually find yourself in known

53
00:03:59,020 --> 00:04:02,500
space - you might even return to your home
planet.

54
00:04:02,500 --> 00:04:05,760
In other words, space may bend into itself.

55
00:04:05,760 --> 00:04:09,599
To understand this, let's look what happens
in two dimensions.

56
00:04:09,599 --> 00:04:13,200
Like many alien races, Earthlings have gone
through a stage in history when they wondered

57
00:04:13,200 --> 00:04:15,560
about the shape of their planet's surface.

58
00:04:15,560 --> 00:04:20,590
Some imagined an infinite surface, whereas
others believed the surface had an edge.

59
00:04:20,590 --> 00:04:22,370
Eventually they found out that both were wrong.

60
00:04:22,370 --> 00:04:25,310
There is no edge, but the surface is finite:

61
00:04:25,310 --> 00:04:28,990
When you sail away into one direction and
keep sailing in a straight line, you will

62
00:04:28,990 --> 00:04:32,520
eventually come back to where you came from.

63
00:04:32,520 --> 00:04:36,000
The reason is, of course, that earth is a
sphere.

64
00:04:36,000 --> 00:04:40,120
Finite surfaces without edge can easily be
understood by 3 dimensional beings.

65
00:04:40,120 --> 00:04:44,590
But how would you explain them to two-dimensional
creatures, like the Flatsnails from Regulus

66
00:04:44,590 --> 00:04:45,850
three?

67
00:04:45,850 --> 00:04:50,340
Flatsnails can only think in two dimensions,
but they have a highly developped portal technology.

68
00:04:50,340 --> 00:04:54,830
A Flatsnail portal alters the topology a surface
by glueing different parts of a surface together

69
00:04:54,830 --> 00:04:57,840
- for example two opposing sides of a square.

70
00:04:57,840 --> 00:05:02,150
When a Flatsnail leaves the rectangle on one
side, she enters on the other side - like

71
00:05:02,150 --> 00:05:03,620
in a video game.

72
00:05:03,620 --> 00:05:08,970
Topologically, this is the same thing as a
cylinder. Let's call it a a video game cylinder.

73
00:05:08,970 --> 00:05:12,350
What would a video game cylinder look like
from the interior?

74
00:05:12,350 --> 00:05:15,919
Flatsnails can't see very far, but if they
could, they would see an endless series of

75
00:05:15,919 --> 00:05:17,470
images of the same space.

76
00:05:17,470 --> 00:05:21,460
A bit like what you see when you stand between
two parallel mirrors.

77
00:05:21,460 --> 00:05:25,220
What happens when you glue the other two sides
of the square together, as well?

78
00:05:25,220 --> 00:05:28,419
Now you have the loopy video game effect in
two directions.

79
00:05:28,419 --> 00:05:32,650
A farsighted Flatsnail inside would see her
own image repeated in a grid pattern.

80
00:05:32,650 --> 00:05:37,979
Topologically, this is a torus - the surface
of a donut. So, let's call the double-loopy

81
00:05:37,979 --> 00:05:40,250
rectangle "video game torus".

82
00:05:40,250 --> 00:05:43,800
The important point is that you don't need
the third dimension to define a cylinder or

83
00:05:43,800 --> 00:05:44,910
a torus.

84
00:05:44,910 --> 00:05:48,100
All you need is portals, to glue the edges
together.

85
00:05:48,100 --> 00:05:52,150
And once the glueing is done, you can forget
about the portals and just enjoy your seamless

86
00:05:52,150 --> 00:05:54,360
yet loopy video game environment.

87
00:05:54,360 --> 00:05:59,430
A Flatsnail that grew up on this torus would
be used to the fact that she lives on a flat,

88
00:05:59,430 --> 00:06:02,400
finite, somewhat loopy surface, without edge.

89
00:06:02,400 --> 00:06:07,490
No need for portals or bendy space, not to
mention this esoterical "third dimension".

90
00:06:07,490 --> 00:06:10,020
But what about the universe we live in ?

91
00:06:10,020 --> 00:06:14,300
Is it so unthinkable that our universe is
similar - only in three dimensions?

92
00:06:14,300 --> 00:06:15,430
Why shouldn't it?

93
00:06:15,430 --> 00:06:19,400
Is there any reason why the universe has to
be either an euklidean space, or embedded

94
00:06:19,400 --> 00:06:22,139
in a higher dimensional euklidean hyperspace?

95
00:06:22,139 --> 00:06:24,080
But back to two dimensions.

96
00:06:24,080 --> 00:06:27,240
Let's toy around some more with surfaces and
portals.

97
00:06:27,240 --> 00:06:31,300
For example, what happens when we glue together
two sides of a rectangle, but with flipped

98
00:06:31,300 --> 00:06:32,190
directions?

99
00:06:32,190 --> 00:06:37,650
Well, we get a Moebius strip - a surface where
each round trip makes you flip orientation.

100
00:06:37,650 --> 00:06:42,020
When you glue the other pair without flipping,
you get a Klein bottle.

101
00:06:42,020 --> 00:06:45,889
When you flip both pairs, you get something
called Projective Plane.

102
00:06:45,889 --> 00:06:49,400
Now, we are not limited to rectangles, though.

103
00:06:49,400 --> 00:06:53,820
We can, for example, take a parallelogram
and glue opposing edges together.

104
00:06:53,820 --> 00:06:57,550
Geometrically this is a skewed torus, topologically
it's the same thing.

105
00:06:57,550 --> 00:07:02,220
A Flatsnail sitting inside would see herself
repeated in a skewed grid patten.

106
00:07:02,220 --> 00:07:06,100
What happens if you take a hexagon and glue
opposing sides together without flipping?

107
00:07:06,100 --> 00:07:10,350
Well, a flatsnail inside would see the herself
in the same pattern as before.

108
00:07:10,350 --> 00:07:14,460
So this is just another way to construct the
same skewed torus.

109
00:07:14,460 --> 00:07:16,259
Let's go back to the video game torus.

110
00:07:16,259 --> 00:07:20,160
Can't we do a similar construction in three-dimensional
space?

111
00:07:20,160 --> 00:07:21,490
Of course we can.

112
00:07:21,490 --> 00:07:26,000
All we have to do is take a cuboid in space,
and link opposing sides with portals without

113
00:07:26,000 --> 00:07:27,479
twisting anything.

114
00:07:27,479 --> 00:07:31,979
If some spacefarer happens to be trapped inside,
he will find himself in a three dimensional

115
00:07:31,979 --> 00:07:35,789
video game space where he comes back to his
origin when he flies long enough along one

116
00:07:35,789 --> 00:07:37,070
of the axis.

117
00:07:37,070 --> 00:07:41,560
He won't even notice the portals - he would
just experience an universe that somehow repeats

118
00:07:41,560 --> 00:07:42,699
itself.

119
00:07:42,699 --> 00:07:45,520
This is called a hyper-torus.

120
00:07:45,520 --> 00:07:50,020
Just like our video game torus, the hypertorus
is "flat", which means that the rules of school

121
00:07:50,020 --> 00:07:53,229
geometry still hold for very large objects.

122
00:07:53,229 --> 00:07:57,020
In part 2 we will define more precisely what
this means.

123
00:07:57,020 --> 00:07:58,580
Did you see what we just did? W

124
00:07:58,580 --> 00:08:02,990
e constructed a flat universe, with finite
volume, but no edge.

125
00:08:02,990 --> 00:08:04,680
Something Hank and Derek thought was impossible.

126
00:08:04,680 --> 00:08:09,410
Such a universe looks exactly like infinite
flat three-dimensional space, except that

127
00:08:09,410 --> 00:08:13,600
it repeats itself like a hotel wallpaper - in
three directions.

128
00:08:13,600 --> 00:08:18,289
Some earthling scientists think that the hyper-torus
is a possible shape of the universe.

129
00:08:18,289 --> 00:08:20,960
"No Edge!"

130
00:08:20,960 --> 00:08:22,630
Yes Earthling, no edge.

131
00:08:22,630 --> 00:08:27,069
But the hypertorus is not the only flat finite
model.

132
00:08:27,069 --> 00:08:32,050
For example, you can obtain another model
by turning the top portal by 90°.

133
00:08:32,050 --> 00:08:34,200
This is called the "quarter-turn manifold".

134
00:08:34,200 --> 00:08:40,479
A third variant is the "half-turn manifold",
obtained by turning the top portal by 180°.

135
00:08:40,479 --> 00:08:43,659
Remember how we constructed a torus with a
honeycomb pattern?

136
00:08:43,659 --> 00:08:46,940
You can do the same with a hexagonal prism
to get the hypertorus.

137
00:08:46,940 --> 00:08:52,339
Now, when you rotate the top gate by 60°
to get the "one-sixth turn manifold", or by

138
00:08:52,339 --> 00:08:56,339
120° to obtain the "one-third turn manifold".

139
00:08:56,339 --> 00:09:01,880
There are overall 10 models for a finite closed
flat universe - 6 orientable and 4 non-orientable

140
00:09:01,880 --> 00:09:02,640
ones.

141
00:09:02,640 --> 00:09:04,730
What does "non-orientable" mean?

142
00:09:04,730 --> 00:09:08,870
It means that the universe is like a moebius
strip: When you make a round trip, you might

143
00:09:08,870 --> 00:09:10,600
come home mirror-inverted.

144
00:09:10,600 --> 00:09:14,100
This is called an Alice Universe. For you,
your planet will be mirror-inverted.

145
00:09:14,100 --> 00:09:19,230
Also, as electrical charge is a matter of
orientation, the planet and everything on

146
00:09:19,230 --> 00:09:21,100
it will consist of antimatter.

147
00:09:21,100 --> 00:09:28,100
But the ten finite, closed, flat models are
not the only possibilities.

148
00:09:28,260 --> 00:09:32,450
In part two and three we will see other, curved
models for the universe, like the Hyper-sphere

149
00:09:32,450 --> 00:09:34,370
or the Poincaré space.

150
00:09:34,370 --> 00:09:38,410
We wil also talk about evidence that may help
finding out which model is correct.

151
00:09:38,410 --> 00:09:41,880
Of course, the crash course for alien visitors
continues.

152
00:09:41,880 --> 00:09:45,990
What do you think: Should I alternate between
the Crash Course and No Edge episodes, or

153
00:09:45,990 --> 00:09:49,260
rather finish this mini-series and then continue
the crash course?

154
00:09:49,260 --> 00:09:54,050
